course: religion-ethics
lesson: 73
title: What other faiths and non-religious people believe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Ellie's RE class every child brings a different answer to the same old question, «What happens when we die?», the way children from different countries bring different dishes to one shared table.
explain: Mohammed says Muslims believe the soul waits for the Day of Judgement, when God judges everyone fairly and with mercy, and good people enter Paradise, called Jannah; Ben says many Jews believe in Olam Ha-Ba, «the World to Come», though Judaism puts most weight on living a good life now.
explain: Priya says Hindus believe the soul is reborn in a new body, a person or an animal, until it is freed from the cycle (moksha); Mei's Buddhist grandma believes in rebirth until a being reaches nirvana, a peace beyond suffering; Gurpreet says Sikhs believe the soul returns to God like a drop returning to the ocean.
explain: Aroha says her Māori grandparents believe the spirit travels to Cape Reinga at the top of New Zealand and leaps into the ocean to join the ancestors; Jack's humanist uncle says «When we die, we just stop; that's why this life is so important»; and Mia says, honestly, «Nobody really knows.»
explain: Christians, like Ellie, believe in resurrection and life with God; the beliefs differ, but all try to answer the same human question, and all value life and remembering.
explain: The rule of the class is simple: you can disagree, ask questions and explain your own view, but you never mock someone's faith or lack of faith.
fact: Muslims believe that after death the soul waits until the Day of Judgement, when God judges everyone fairly; the good go to Paradise, called Jannah.
fact: Muslims usually bury the dead quickly, often within a day, and pray for them.
fact: Many Jews believe in an afterlife called Olam Ha-Ba, the World to Come, but Judaism focuses mostly on living a good life now; mourners say the Kaddish prayer.
fact: Hindus believe the soul is reborn in a new body (reincarnation) until it is freed from the cycle of rebirth; this freedom is called moksha.
fact: Buddhists believe beings are reborn until they reach nirvana, a state of peace and freedom from suffering.
fact: Many Buddhists teach that what is reborn is not a fixed soul but a flow of life, like a flame lit from another candle.
fact: Sikhs believe the soul is reborn until it becomes one with God, like a drop returning to the ocean; Sikhs usually cremate the dead and sing hymns.
fact: Many Māori believe the spirit travels to Cape Reinga, at the top of New Zealand, and into the ocean to the ancestors; many Māori are also Christian.
fact: Humanists believe there is no life after death, so they find meaning in living well, loving others and leaving good memories.
fact: An agnostic is someone who says we cannot know for sure what happens after death.
fact: Christians believe in resurrection and eternal life with God.
fact: Mocking someone's beliefs is unkind; people can disagree with respect.
fact: All faiths and philosophies try to make sense of death, and all value life and remembering.
exercise: What do Muslims believe happens after death? | The soul waits for the Day of Judgement | The soul is reborn as an animal at once | Nothing at all happens after death | Everyone becomes an angel straight away
exercise: What is Jannah in Islam? | Paradise | A prayer for the dead | A holy book | A festival
exercise: What is Olam Ha-Ba, in which many Jews believe? | The World to Come | A Jewish wedding song | The Jewish New Year | A kind of bread
exercise: What does Judaism focus on most? | Living a good life now | Getting ready for being reborn | Travelling to a sacred cape | Avoiding all talk of death
exercise: What prayer do Jewish mourners say? | The Kaddish | The Lord's Prayer | The Hail Mary | The Creed
exercise: Priya says Hindus believe in reincarnation. What is it? | The soul being reborn in a new body | The body rising from the grave | The soul resting in a garden for ever | The person becoming an angel
exercise: What is moksha in Hinduism? | Freedom from the cycle of rebirth | The first life a soul ever has | A Hindu festival of lights | A prayer said at a wedding
exercise: Mei's Buddhist grandma believes in nirvana. What is it? | A peace beyond suffering, the end of rebirth | A heaven full of gold and jewels, where good Buddhists live like kings | A Buddhist temple in China | A kind of meditation mat
exercise: How do many Buddhists describe what is reborn? | Not a fixed soul, but like a flame lit from another | Exactly the same person, with all the same memories and the same name | A ghost that floats into a new house | Nothing; Buddhists don't believe in rebirth
exercise: How does Gurpreet describe the Sikh belief about the soul? | It returns to God like a drop to the ocean | It turns into a star in the night sky | It sleeps under the ground for ever | It becomes a guardian angel at once
exercise: Where do many Māori believe the spirit travels after death? | To Cape Reinga and into the ocean to the ancestors | To the top of Mount Everest | To a city of gold in the clouds | To the moon, where it lives for ever
exercise: Jack's uncle is a humanist. What does he believe about death? | It's the end, so this life matters a lot | The soul is reborn as a tree in the place where the person is buried | Everyone goes to a happy heaven with all their family and pets | Dead people become ghosts who stay near the people they loved
exercise: Mia says, «Nobody really knows what happens.» What is this view called? | Agnostic | Atheist | Humanist | Hindu
exercise: Is it okay to say «I don't know» about what happens after death? | Yes; many honest people say that | No; everyone must pick one answer | No; it is rude to all religions | Only grown-ups are allowed to say it
exercise: A boy mocks Priya for believing in reincarnation. What is right? | It's unkind; people can disagree without mocking | He's right to laugh, because it is a strange belief and she should know it | Priya should stop talking about her faith | Teachers should ban talk about religion
exercise: What do all these beliefs have in common? | They try to make sense of death and value life | They all say exactly the same thing | They all believe in being reborn | They all say nothing happens after death
test: «Coming back as a cat? That's mad jokes,» laughs a lad in Liverpool (mad jokes = really funny) when Priya explains reincarnation. What's the right response? | Disagree if you like, but don't mock her faith | Laugh along with him, because it is a strange idea after all | Priya should keep her religion at home | Tell the teacher Priya is lying
test: The vicar says, very formally: «We hold our own hope, yet we accord our neighbours of other faiths the fullest respect.» What does he mean? | Christians keep their faith and respect others | Other faiths are wrong and must be corrected at once, kindly but firmly | All faiths are exactly the same as ours, in every way | Other faiths should not be discussed at all in church
test: «My uncle's a humanist, he reckons when you die you just stop. Is that sad tho?» asks Jack. What do humanists say? | It makes this life matter more: love and live well | They are sad all the time about it and can never enjoy themselves | They secretly believe in heaven | They think life has no meaning
test: Aroha from Auckland says, «Chur, my koro told me about Cape Reinga» (chur = thanks/cool, koro = grandad). What does Cape Reinga mean in Māori belief? | Where spirits leave for the ocean and the ancestors | Where Māori go on summer holiday | Where babies are born in the old stories of the Māori people | Where the first church in New Zealand stood
test: What would happen if everyone in RE mocked each other's beliefs? | People would get hurt and stop sharing | Everyone would learn faster | All beliefs would become the same | Nothing; beliefs don't matter to people
test: «Do Jews believe in heaven or nah?» asks a boy in New Jersey. What's the most accurate answer? | Many believe in the World to Come, but focus on this life | No Jew believes anything about after death | Yes, exactly the same heaven as Christians | No, Jews believe in reincarnation as cows
test: «Nirvana's that old band my dad likes, innit?» says a girl in Essex. What does it mean for Buddhists? | A peace beyond suffering where rebirth ends | A rock band's favourite holy place, where they play | A heaven full of gold, sweets and loud music | The name of a famous Buddhist king who lived long ago
test: A Gen Z cousin says, «Islam's lowkey the same as Christianity about death, right?» What's accurate? | Both believe in God's judgement, but teach differently about Jesus | They are exactly the same in every way, about death and about Jesus too | Islam teaches reincarnation instead | Islam teaches that nothing happens at all
test: In a cartoon, every dead character goes up a golden staircase to the same cloud. What's more accurate? | Faiths and non-religious people believe different things | Every faith agrees on the golden staircase | Scientists have proved the staircase exists, using telescopes and satellites | Only cartoon characters ever die
test: «Gurpreet said the soul goes back to God like a drop to the sea. That's lush, actually,» says a girl in Swansea (lush = lovely). Which faith teaches this? | Sikhism | Islam | Humanism | Judaism
test: Myth to check: «If you respect someone's faith, you have to agree with it.» | False; you can respect and still disagree | True; respect means believing the very same thing | True, but only during RE lessons at school | False, because respect is not needed for beliefs at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Muslim teaching concerning death? | The soul awaits the Day of Judgement, whereupon the righteous enter Paradise | The soul is reborn forthwith in the body of an animal | Existence ceases entirely and permanently at death | The departed become angels immediately upon death
exam: Identify the term employed in Hinduism to denote liberation from the cycle of rebirth. | Moksha | Nirvana | Jannah | Olam Ha-Ba
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning humanist views of death. | Humanists hold that life ends at death and locate meaning in living well | Humanists hold that the soul is reborn according to karma | Humanists hold that the dead await a Day of Judgement | Humanists hold that the dead become spirits of nature
exam: Whereas pupils hold differing beliefs concerning death, ascertain the conduct most consistent with the matters taught. | Disagreement expressed with respect and without mockery | Mockery of beliefs which one does not share, provided it is done in jest among friends | Compulsory adoption of a single belief by all | Prohibition of all discussion of belief in school
